/**
 * @mainpage Register Allocator Architecture & Implementation
 *
 * @section overview Project Overview
 * This project implements a sophisticated **Graph-Coloring Register Allocation** suite designed for modern compiler backends. 
 * It transforms variable live ranges into a **Web-based Interference Graph** and provides four distinct allocation strategies 
 * ranging from simple greedy heuristics to advanced hybrid models with exhaustive structural optimization.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section usage Usage Instructions
 *
 * @subsection build Building the Project
 * The project uses a standard `Makefile`. To build the executable:
 * @code{.bash}
 * make
 * @endcode
 * This will generate the `myProg` binary in the root directory.
 *
 * @subsection batch_mode Batch Mode
 * Designed for automated processing and performance evaluation.
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./myProg -b <ranges_file> <config_file> <output_file>
 * @endcode
 * - **ranges_file**: Path to the variable live ranges.
 * - **config_file**: Path to the register and algorithm configuration.
 * - **output_file**: Path where the allocation report will be saved.
 *
 * @subsection interactive_mode Interactive Mode
 * Provides a user-friendly menu to load files and select algorithms manually.
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./myProg
 * @endcode
 * Follow the on-screen prompts to configure and run the allocation.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section testing Testing and Validation
 * The project includes a comprehensive automated test suite to ensure the technical integrity and output correctness of all allocation algorithms.
 *
 * @subsection run_tests Running the Tests
 * You can execute the test suite using the provided script:
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./tests/run_tests.sh all        # Run the full suite (88 test cases)
 * ./tests/run_tests.sh smoke      # Run only error-handling/smoke tests
 * ./tests/run_tests.sh free       # Run only Free algorithm tests
 * ./tests/run_tests.sh ranges     # Run all ranges against all algorithms
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection validation Validation Criteria
 * A test is marked as **Passed** only if it satisfies the following structural requirements:
 * 1.  **Zero-Crash Execution:** The program must exit with code 0.
 * 2.  **Artifact Generation:** The script verifies the creation of the report (.txt), the interference graph (.gv), and the allocated graph (_allocated.gv).
 * 3.  **Format Compliance:** The output text is scanned via grep to ensure it strictly follows the project specification.
 * 4.  **Graph Validity:** DOT files are checked for valid Graphviz headers to ensure visualization compatibility.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section architectures Allocation Architectures
 *
 * @subsection basic 1. Basic Allocator (Greedy Simplification)
 * The foundational algorithm based on Chaitin's graph simplification.
 * - **Core Principle:** A graph is \f$N\f$-colorable if we can iteratively remove nodes with degree \f$< N\f$.
 * - **Process:** Nodes with degree \f$< N\f$ are pushed onto a stack.
 * - **Failure Mode:** If all remaining nodes have degree \f$\geq N\f$, the allocator terminates as unfeasible.
 * - **Coloring:** Pops nodes from the stack and assigns the smallest available register index [0, N-1].
 *
 * @subsection spilling 2. Spilling Allocator (Load/Store Fallback)
 * Extends the Basic Allocator with a recovery strategy to ensure 100% completion rate.
 * - **Recovery Logic:** When stuck (all nodes \f$\geq N\f$), it selects a "victim" node to move to memory (Spill).
 * - **Heuristic:**
 *   - **Primary:** Highest Degree (removes the most interference edges).
 *   - **Secondary (Tie-breaker):** Smallest Footprint (minimizes data movement costs by picking nodes with fewer definitions and last uses).
 * - **Result:** The victim is marked as Memory (M) and the graph is simplified again.
 *
 * @subsection splitting 3. Splitting Allocator (Structural Optimization)
 * A structural alternative to spilling that attempts to keep variables in registers by fragmenting their live ranges.
 * - **Exhaustive Brute-Force Search:** Instead of arbitrary mid-point splitting, this implementation runs a **full simulation** for every possible split point.
 * - **The "Best Point" Heuristic:** For each potential split, it rebuilds the graph and runs a "Simplified Colorability Test."
 * - **Evaluation Metric:** It selects the split point that minimizes the number of "stuck" nodes (degree \f$\geq N\f$).
 * - **Cycle Breaking:** This approach is uniquely capable of breaking complex interference **Cycles** (like the 5-cycle) without requiring any memory spills.
 *
 * @subsection free 4. Free (Hybrid) Allocator (Expert Decision Tree)
 * The most intelligent tier, combining global analysis with high-end heuristics.
 *
 * **The Decision Loop:**
 * 1.  **Clique Detection (Fail-Fast):** Runs a greedy clique finder. If a clique of size \f$N+1\f$ is found at any program point, it spills the node with the **highest degree** within that clique instantly.
 * 2.  **DSATUR Coloring:** Uses the **Degree of Saturation** algorithm. It prioritizes nodes based on neighbor constraints, finding optimal colorings for dense graphs where greedy ordering fails.
 * 3.  **Advanced Splitting Fallback:** If DSATUR fails to color all nodes, it triggers the **Exhaustive Brute-Force Split** logic for the uncolored node with the highest degree.
 * 4.  **Surgical Spill:** If the split budget (limit of 3) is exhausted or splitting doesn't improve colorability, it performs a targeted spill and restarts.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section complexity Complexity Summary
 *
 * The performance of each allocator is determined by the number of variables (\f$W\f$), the length of live ranges (\f$L\f$), and the total number of interferences (\f$E\f$).
 *
 * | Algorithm | Complexity | Strategy | Best Used For |
 * | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
 * | **Basic** | \f$O(W^2 \cdot L \log L + E)\f$ | Simple Greedy | Low-pressure, linear code |
 * | **Spilling** | \f$O(W^2 \cdot L \log L + W^2 + E)\f$ | Degree-based Spill | Reliable general-purpose allocation |
 * | **Splitting** | \f$O(S \cdot L^2 W^2 \log L)\f$ | Brute-Force Simulation | Breaking complex interference cycles |
 * | **Free** | \f$O(C \cdot W^3 + S_{limit} \cdot L^2 W^2 \log L)\f$ | Hybrid Decision Tree | Maximum register utilization |
 *
 * For a more granular breakdown of internal functions (like `interferesWith` or `findGreedyClique`), please refer to the project's [ALLOCATORS.md](./ALLOCATORS.md).
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section examples Visual Examples (5-Cycle Case)
 *
 * Below is a demonstration of how the **Splitting Allocator** resolves an uncolorable 5-cycle graph (\f$N=2\f$) by surgically breaking a live range.
 *
 * @subsection uncolored Initial Interference Graph
 * The original graph forms a pentagon (cycle of 5 nodes). With only 2 registers, this graph is uncolorable.
 *
 * @dot
 * graph { 
 * labelloc  = top;
 * labeljust = left;
 * fontname  = calibri;
 * fontsize  = 16;
 * rankdir = LR;
 *   node [shape=circle, style=filled];
 * 
 *   0;
 *   0 -- 1;
 *   0 -- 4;
 *   1;
 *   1 -- 2;
 *   2;
 *   2 -- 3;
 *   3;
 *   3 -- 4;
 *   4;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * @subsection colored Allocated Graph (After Splitting)
 * The allocator splits Web 0 into two fragments (Web 0 and Web 5), breaking the cycle and allowing a feasible 2-register allocation.
 *
 * @dot
 * graph {
 *   labelloc = top;
 *   labeljust = left;
 *   fontname = calibri;
 *   fontsize = 16;
 *   pad = 0.45;
 *   nodesep = 0.9;
 *   ranksep = 1.15;
 *   rankdir = LR;
 *   node [shape=circle, style=filled];
 * 
 *   subgraph cluster_legend {
 *     label="Allocation legend";
 *     legend_node [shape=plain, label=<
 *       <TABLE BORDER="1" CELLBORDER="1" CELLSPACING="0">
 *         <TR><TD COLSPAN="4"><B>Run summary</B></TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD>Algorithm</TD><TD COLSPAN="3">splitting</TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD>Registers used</TD><TD>2</TD><TD>Feasible</TD><TD>yes</TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD>Splits</TD><TD>1</TD><TD>Spills</TD><TD>0</TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD COLSPAN="4"><B>Web details</B></TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD BGCOLOR="#4ECDC4">web1</TD><TD>v2</TD><TD>2,3</TD><TD>r1</TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD BGCOLOR="#FF6B6B">web2</TD><TD>v3</TD><TD>3,4</TD><TD>r0</TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD BGCOLOR="#4ECDC4">web3</TD><TD>v4</TD><TD>4,5</TD><TD>r1</TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD BGCOLOR="#FF6B6B">web4</TD><TD>v5</TD><TD>1,5</TD><TD>r0</TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD BGCOLOR="#4ECDC4">web0</TD><TD>v1</TD><TD>1</TD><TD>r1</TD></TR>
 *         <TR><TD BGCOLOR="#FF6B6B">web5</TD><TD>v1</TD><TD>2,100,101</TD><TD>r0</TD></TR>
 *       </TABLE>>];
 *   }
 *   web1 [label="web1 (v2)", fillcolor="#4ECDC4"];
 *   web2 [label="web2 (v3)", fillcolor="#FF6B6B"];
 *   web3 [label="web3 (v4)", fillcolor="#4ECDC4"];
 *   web4 [label="web4 (v5)", fillcolor="#FF6B6B"];
 *   web0 [label="web0 (v1)", fillcolor="#4ECDC4"];
 *   web5 [label="web5 (v1)", fillcolor="#FF6B6B"];
 *   web0 -- web4;
 *   web1 -- web2;
 *   web1 -- web5;
 *   web2 -- web3;
 *   web3 -- web4;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section range_files Additional Range Files
 *
 * The project includes several range files for testing specific allocator behaviors:
 *
 * | File | Purpose |
 * | :--- | :--- |
 * | `ranges_cycle_split.txt` | **5-Cycle Split Case:** Pentagon interference graph requiring 3 colors (breakable with N=2 by splitting). |
 * | `ranges_pres1_dsatur.txt` | **Star Graph (DSATUR Superiority):** Demonstrates DSATUR's advantage over simple greedy ordering. |
 * | `ranges_pres2_split.txt` | **Surgical Split (Hybrid Resolution):** Splits long-lived variables at natural gaps to resolve conflicts. |
 * | `ranges_pres3_clique.txt` | **Point Clique (Fail-Fast Detection):** Detects K4 cliques and bypasses redundant coloring for N=3. |
 * | `ranges_split_win.txt` | **Split Win Case:** Resolves boundary conflicts between high and low pressure regions. |
 * | `ranges7.txt` | **Stress Test (K10 Clique):** Dense interference graph with 10 variables overlapping at line 1000. |
 * | `ranges8.txt` | **Congestion Test (Local Pressure):** Complex overlapping ranges with specific congestion points. |
 */
